			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-462339">
									

<p>I did mention that idea to Spoon in chat when he first brought the SCP up, but he felt that it was okay to say the SCP doesn't work that way.</p>
<p>I think this is partially justified — you could argue that the effect is somehow gravitationally locked to the planet. The big leap is then the fact that it's stationary in a nonrotating frame of reference attached to the center of the Earth. As a curvature in spacetime the SCP should probably move or dissipate in some fashion in that frame.</p>
<p>In a way, you can tie the mystery of its rock solid position to the mystery of what keeps the SCP from changing shape or dissolving apart in gravitational waves in the first place pretty nicely. There's some pretty strange exotic matter things going on behind the scenes.</p>

